# Cosmic-Lang Formal Grammar (EBNF)

| | |
|---|---|
| **Status** | **Proposed — Phase 1 draft.** Not approved. |
| **Phase** | 1 (formal specification) |
| **Directive** | ENGINEERING DIRECTIVE 002, deliverable D2 |
| **Legacy reference** | `CosmicLang/cosmcc` @ `284358f43db885ccd8b52b11f897ac0defec10de` (v0.6.5) |
| **Companion documents** | `docs/spec/language-spec.md` (D1), `docs/spec/typing-contracts.md` (D3), `docs/spec/decisions/` (D4) |

## 0. Evidence policy

**Every production below carries an evidence marker.** A production without
evidence does not appear.

| Marker | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `[E1:path]` | Derived directly from the legacy source at the frozen SHA. The path is given relative to `kof-compiler/src/main/java/dev/kof/compiler/`. |
| `[E1:case]` | Witnessed by an executed golden case (`tests/golden/<case>/`), executed 222/222 for jvm/js/script per `docs/audits/phase-0-baseline.md` §5.2. |
| `[E2]` | Inferred from E1 material; the inference is stated. |
| `[UNRESOLVED:Sn]` | Deliberately not decided. Owned by decision record `Sn`. The production is **provisional** and must not be implemented as if settled. |

**No legacy code or fixture text is reproduced.** Where a form is illustrated,
the example is written for this document; the evidence marker names the source
of the *form*, not of the example.

**This grammar is not yet a contract.** Productions marked `[UNRESOLVED]` are
placeholders that record the shape observed in the legacy parser so that Phase 2
can be held to a decision rather than to an accident.

---

## 1. Notation

ISO-style EBNF: `=` definition · `,` concatenation · `|` alternation ·
`[ ]` optional · `{ }` zero or more · `( )` grouping · `"…"` terminal ·
`(* … *)` comment.

`(*§n*)` cross-references `docs/spec/language-spec.md` section *n*.

---

## 2. Lexical grammar

### 2.1 Source characters and layout

```ebnf
(*§1.1*) source        = [ bom ] , { character } ;
          bom           = "\uFEFF" ;

(*§1.2*) line-terminator = "\n" | "\r\n" | "\r" ;
(*§1.4*) whitespace      = " " | "\t" | "\f" ;            (* E1:parser/Lexer.java tokenize *)

(*§2.1*) comment         = line-comment | block-comment ;
          line-comment  = "//" , { any-char - line-terminator } ;
          block-comment = "/*" , { any-char } , "*/" ;    (* non-nesting: E1:parser/Lexer.java skipBlockComment; UNRESOLVED:S14 *)
```

### 2.2 Identifiers

```ebnf
(*§2.2*) identifier = identifier-start , { identifier-part } ;
          identifier-start = unicode-letter | "_" | "$" ;  (* E1:parser/Lexer.java tokenize *)
          identifier-part  = unicode-letter | unicode-digit | "_" | "$" ;
                                                           (* E1:parser/Lexer.java readIdentifier *)
```

`unicode-letter` / `unicode-digit` follow the Unicode general categories used by
the legacy lexer (`Character.isLetter` / `isLetterOrDigit`), i.e. they are
**Unicode-aware, not ASCII-only**. `[E2]` because the corpus exercises ASCII
identifiers only. `[UNRESOLVED:S13]` owns the exact class set and normalisation.

### 2.3 Keywords

```ebnf
(*§2.3*) keyword = "class" | "interface" | "record" | "enum" | "entity"
                 | "extern" | "generated" | "audited" | "unique"
                 | "extends" | "implements"
                 | "package" | "import"
                 | "public" | "private" | "protected" | "static" | "final"
                 | "abstract" | "transient" | "volatile" | "synchronized"
                 | "native" | "default" | "override"
                 | "void" | "new" | "this" | "super"
                 | "return" | "throw" | "if" | "else" | "for" | "while" | "do"
                 | "switch" | "case" | "break" | "continue"
                 | "try" | "catch" | "finally"
                 | "spawn" | "await" | "assert" | "instanceof" | "var" | "val" | "as"
                 | "bool" | "byte" | "short" | "int" | "long"
                 | "float" | "double" | "char" | "string"
                 | "true" | "false" | "null" ;             (* E1:lang/KofKeywords.java *)

(*§2.3*) reserved-invalid = "fun" | "fn" | "func" ;        (* E1:lang/KofKeywords.java (SG-001); parser rejects *)
```

`reserved-invalid` tokens are lexed and then **rejected by the parser**; they
must never be accepted as identifiers. `[E1:parser/Parser.java parse()]`

### 2.4 Literals

```ebnf
(*§2.4*) literal        = int-literal | long-literal | float-literal
                        | double-literal | string-literal | char-literal
                        | boolean-literal | null-literal ;

          int-literal    = decimal-int | hex-int ;
          long-literal   = ( decimal-int | hex-int ) , ( "l" | "L" ) ;
          decimal-int    = digit , { digit } ;
          hex-int        = ( "0x" | "0X" ) , hex-digit , { hex-digit } ;
                                                           (* E1:parser/Lexer.java readNumber *)

          float-literal  = number-body , ( "f" | "F" ) ;
          double-literal = number-body , [ ( "d" | "D" ) ] ;
          number-body    = decimal-int ,
                           [ "." , digit , { digit } ] ,   (* fraction only if a digit follows the "." *)
                           [ ( "e" | "E" ) , [ "+" | "-" ] , digit , { digit } ] ;
                                                           (* E1:parser/Lexer.java readNumber *)
(*§2.4*)  (* NOT part of the language: binary/octal literals, digit separators,
             trailing ".", hex floats, leading-"." floats, "~" operator.       *)
                                                           (* E1: negative evidence, Lexer.java + TokenType.java *)

(*§2.4*) string-literal = '"' , { string-char | escape } , '"' ;
          string-char    = any-char - ( '"' | "\" ) ;
          char-literal   = "'" , ( char-value ) , "'" ;
          char-value     = escape | ( any-char - ( "'" | "\" | line-terminator ) ) ;

(*§2.4*) escape         = "\" , ( "n" | "t" | "r" | "\" | "'" | '"' | "0" | unicode-escape )
                        | "\" , any-char ;                 (* unknown escape yields the character itself,
                                                              with NO diagnostic: E1:parser/Lexer.java readEscape *)
          unicode-escape = "u" , hex-digit , hex-digit , hex-digit , hex-digit ;
                                                           (* E1:parser/Lexer.java readUnicodeEscape *)

(*§2.4*) boolean-literal = "true" | "false" ;               (* E1:lang/KofKeywords.java *)
(*§2.4*) null-literal    = "null" ;

(*§2.4*) (* EXPLICITLY ABSENT: triple-quoted strings. The legacy lexer recognises
           '"""' only to reject it with LEX008. There is no raw/multiline string
           literal and no interpolation. [E1:parser/Lexer.java skipTripleQuoted]
           [UNRESOLVED:S8] owns whether the new language keeps the rejection. *)
```

### 2.5 Operators and delimiters

```ebnf
(*§2.5*) operator   = "+" | "-" | "*" | "/" | "%"
                   | "==" | "!=" | "<" | "<=" | ">" | ">="
                   | "&&" | "||" | "!"
                   | "&" | "|" | "^" | "<<" | ">>" | ">>>"
                   | "=" | "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/=" | "%="
                   | "&=" | "|=" | "^=" | "<<=" | ">>=" | ">>>="
                   | "++" | "--" | "->" ;
                                                           (* E1:parser/Lexer.java readOperatorOrDelimiter *)

(*§2.5*) delimiter  = "(" | ")" | "{" | "}" | "[" | "]" ;
(*§2.5*) separator  = ";" | "," | "." | ":" | "?" | "@" ;
```

Longest-match applies: `>>>` before `>>` before `>`, `>>>=` before `>>>`.
`[E1:parser/Lexer.java readOperatorOrDelimiter]`

---

## 3. Syntactic grammar

### 3.1 Compilation unit

```ebnf
(*§4.1*) compilation-unit = [ package-decl ] , { import-decl } , { top-level-decl } ;
                                                           (* E1:parser/Parser.java parse() *)
(*§4.1*) package-decl     = "package" , qualified-name ;   (* UNRESOLVED:S9 — optionality and placement *)
(*§4.1*) import-decl      = "import" , qualified-name ;    (* UNRESOLVED:S9 — wildcards, aliases, static imports *)
          qualified-name   = identifier , { "." , identifier } ;
```

### 3.2 Top-level declarations

The dispatch order is significant: each alternative is tested in sequence and
the first match wins. `[E1:parser/Parser.java parse()]`

```ebnf
(*§4.2*) top-level-decl = { annotation } , top-level-body ;

(*§4.2*) top-level-body =
              test-decl                 (* contextual: word "test" + STRING_LITERAL *)
            | application-decl          (* contextual: word "application" + "{" *)
            | infra-decl                (* contextual: word "infra" + STRING_LITERAL *)
            | extern-decl               (* "extern" *)
            | foreign-module-decl       (* contextual: "foreign" "module" *)
            | type-decl                 (* includes the contextual "sealed" prefix *)
            | function-decl             (* identifier | "void" | primitive-type | "(" *)
            ;

(*§2.3*) (* "fun" | "fn" | "func" at this position is REJECTED, not parsed. *)
```

`test`, `application`, `infra`, `foreign`, `module` and `sealed` are
**contextual** keywords recognised by word plus one-token lookahead. `[E1]`
Consequence `[E2]`: they remain valid identifiers in every other position, so
the grammar must encode the lookahead rather than reserving them.

```ebnf
(*§4.2*) test-decl          = "test" , string-literal , block ;      (* UNRESOLVED:S10 *)
(*§4.2*) application-decl   = "application" , block ;                (* UNRESOLVED:S10 *)
(*§4.2*) infra-decl         = "infra" , string-literal , block ;      (* UNRESOLVED:S10 *)
(*§4.2*) extern-decl        = "extern" , function-signature ;         (* UNRESOLVED:S10 *)
(*§4.2*) foreign-module-decl = "foreign" , "module" , string-literal , block ;
                                                                    (* UNRESOLVED:S10; desugars to extern-decls *)
```

### 3.3 Functions

```ebnf
(*§4.3*) function-decl   = [ return-type ] , identifier , parameter-list , function-body ;
          return-type   = type-ref ;                        (* omitted for a void/no-result function *)
          parameter-list = "(" , [ parameter , { "," , parameter } ] , ")" ;
          parameter     = identifier , ":" , type-ref ;
          function-body = block | ( "=" , expression ) ;
                                                           (* E1:parser/Parser.java parseFunctionDeclaration;
                                                              E1:cases hello (no return type, block body),
                                                              functions (block and "=" bodies) *)
(*§4.3*) nested-function-decl = function-decl ;             (* E1:parser/StatementParser.java parseNestedFunction
                                                              UNRESOLVED:S11 — capture semantics *)
```

`[UNRESOLVED:S1]` — whether a parameter type may be omitted, and what the
return-type default is when absent (S1 owns the inference boundary). The corpus
shows the *absence* of a return type for `main`, but not the rule.

### 3.4 Types

```ebnf
(*§4.4*) type-ref        = nominal-type , [ "?" ] , { array-suffix } ;
          nominal-type  = qualified-name , [ type-arguments ] ;
          type-arguments = "<" , type-ref , { "," , type-ref } , ">" ;
          array-suffix  = "[" , "]" ;
                                                           (* E1:parser/TypeParser.java;
                                                              E1:cases nullable-suffix-leading-return-type,
                                                              nullable-soft-cast, nested-generic-leading-return-type *)

(*§4.4*) primitive-type  = "bool" | "byte" | "short" | "int" | "long"
                        | "float" | "double" | "char" | "string" ;
                                                           (* E1:lang/KofKeywords.java *)
```

`[UNRESOLVED:S1]` — nested type-argument resolution
(`Box<Box<Int>>`) is a known legacy defect area and is **not** specified.
`[UNRESOLVED:S2]` — the meaning of `?` beyond the suffix position.
`[E2]` — the `?` suffix is evidenced by the corpus; a *prefix* `?` is not.

### 3.5 Type declarations

```ebnf
(*§4.4*) type-decl       = [ "sealed" ] , ( class-decl | interface-decl
                        | record-decl | enum-decl | entity-decl ) ;

          class-decl     = "class" , identifier , [ supertypes ] , class-body ;
          interface-decl = "interface" , identifier , [ supertypes ] , class-body ;
          entity-decl    = "entity" , identifier , [ supertypes ] , class-body ;
                                                           (* UNRESOLVED:S10 — entity semantics *)

          supertypes     = [ "extends" , type-ref ] ,
                           [ "implements" , type-ref , { "," , type-ref } ] ;

(*§4.4*) record-decl     = "record" , identifier , ( positional-record | record-body ) ;
          positional-record = "(" , [ record-component , { "," , record-component } ] , ")" ;
          record-component  = type-ref , identifier ;
                                                           (* E1:case records — positional form has no braces,
                                                              accessors are called as value.x() *)

(*§4.4*) enum-decl       = "enum" , identifier , "{" ,
                            [ enum-constant , { "," , enum-constant } ] , "}" ;
          enum-constant  = identifier , [ "(" , [ expression , { "," , expression } ] , ")" ] ;
                                                           (* E1:cases enum, enum-methods, enum-switch-expr *)

(*§4.4*) class-body      = "{" , { member } , "}" ;
(*§4.4*) member          = { annotation } , { modifier } ,
                          ( constructor-decl | field-decl | function-decl | type-decl ) ;
          constructor-decl = [ "public" ] , "constructor" , parameter-list , block ;
                                                           (* E1:case classes — "public constructor(...)" with
                                                              "super(...)" as the first statement *)
(*§4.4*) field-decl      = type-ref , identifier , [ "=" , expression ] ;
          modifier        = "public" | "private" | "protected" | "static" | "final"
                          | "abstract" | "transient" | "volatile" | "synchronized"
                          | "native" | "default" | "override" ;
                                                           (* UNRESOLVED:S10 — legal combinations unknown *)
(*§4.4*) annotation      = "@" , qualified-name , [ "(" , [ annotation-args ] , ")" ] ;
          annotation-args = expression , { "," , expression } ;
                                                           (* UNRESOLVED:S10 — vocabulary and semantics *)
```

### 3.6 Statements

```ebnf
(*§5*) statement = block
                 | return-stmt | throw-stmt
                 | if-stmt | while-stmt | do-while-stmt | for-stmt
                 | try-stmt | switch-stmt
                 | var-decl-stmt | nested-function-decl
                 | expression-stmt
                 | ";" ;                                   (* E1:parser/StatementParser.java parseStatement *)

          block        = "{" , { statement } , "}" ;

          return-stmt  = "return" , [ expression ] ;
          throw-stmt   = "throw" , expression ;
                                                           (* E1:case exceptions — "throw \"…\"" throws a string *)

          if-stmt      = "if" , "(" , expression , ")" , statement ,
                         [ "else" , statement ] ;
          while-stmt   = "while" , "(" , expression , ")" , statement ;
          do-while-stmt = "do" , statement , "while" , "(" , expression , ")" ;
          for-stmt     = "for" , "(" , [ for-init ] , ";" , [ expression ] , ";"
                         , [ expression ] , ")" , statement
                       | "for" , "(" , [ "var" | "val" ] , identifier , "in"
                         , expression , ")" , statement ;
                                                           (* E1:case interfaces — "for (var a in animals)".
                                                              The three-clause form is E1 from the parser but is
                                                              NOT exercised by the corpus: [UNRESOLVED:S5] *)

          try-stmt     = "try" , block , { catch-clause } , [ finally-clause ] ;
          catch-clause = "catch" , "(" , type-ref , identifier , ")" , block ;
          finally-clause = "finally" , block ;
                                                           (* E1:cases exceptions, finally-control-flow,
                                                              finally-return *)

          switch-stmt  = "switch" , "(" , expression , ")" , "{" , { switch-arm } , "}" ;
          switch-arm   = "case" , switch-label , [ guard ] , "->" , switch-body ;
          switch-label = expression | type-ref | "default" ;
          guard        = "if" , expression ;
          switch-body  = expression | block | statement ;
                                                           (* E1:parser/StatementParser.java
                                                              parseSwitchStatement, parseSwitchCasePatternOrValue,
                                                              parseGuardIfPresent; E1:cases enum-switch-expr,
                                                              switch-expr, switch-break-in-loop, sealed-switch *)

          var-decl-stmt = ( "var" | "val" ) , identifier , [ ":" , type-ref ] , "=" , expression ;
                                                           (* E1:parser/StatementParser.java parseVarDecl;
                                                              E1:case concurrency-spawn-await *)
          expression-stmt = expression ;
```

`[UNRESOLVED:S5]` — exhaustiveness, whether arms may fall through, and whether
`break`/`continue` accept labels.

### 3.7 Expressions

```ebnf
(*§6.1*) expression   = assignment-expr ;
          assignment-expr = postfix-expr ,
                            [ assignment-op , assignment-expr ] ;   (* associativity: UNRESOLVED:S11 *)
          assignment-op  = "=" | "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/=" | "%="
                         | "&=" | "|=" | "^=" | "<<=" | ">>=" | ">>>=" ;
                                                           (* E1:parser/ExpressionParser.java parseAssignment *)

(*§6.1*) (* Precedence climbing; the table is E1 from ExpressionParser.precedence(). *)
          binary-expr(min) = unary-expr , { binary-op , binary-rhs } ;
          binary-op      = "||"                      (* level 1, lowest *)
                         | "&&"                      (* level 2 *)
                         | "|" | "^"                 (* level 3 *)
                         | "&"                       (* level 4 *)
                         | "==" | "!=" | "<" | "<=" | ">" | ">=" | "instanceof" | "as"   (* level 5 *)
                         | "<<" | ">>" | ">>>"       (* level 6 *)
                         | "+" | "-"                 (* level 7 *)
                         | "*" | "/" | "%" ;         (* level 8, highest *)
          binary-rhs     = type-ref                   (* only for "as" and "instanceof" *)
                         | binary-expr(level + 1) ;
                                                           (* E1:parser/ExpressionParser.java parseBinary *)

(*§6.1*) unary-expr   = ( "!" | "-" | "++" | "--" ) , unary-expr
                     | "spawn" , unary-expr
                     | "await" , unary-expr
                     | postfix-expr ;
                                                           (* E1:parser/ExpressionParser.java parseUnary *)

(*§6.1*) postfix-expr = primary , { postfix-op } ;
          postfix-op = "(" , [ argument-list ] , ")"
                     | "." , identifier , [ "(" , [ argument-list ] , ")" ]   (* method or property *)
                     | "[" , expression , "]"
                     | "++" | "--"
                     | type-arguments , "(" , [ argument-list ] , ")" ;        (* generic call *)
                                                           (* E1:parser/ExpressionParser.java parsePostfix,
                                                              parseCallTypeArguments *)
          argument-list = expression , { "," , expression } ;

(*§6.1*) primary      = literal
                     | identifier
                     | "this" | "super"
                     | "(" , expression , ")"
                     | lambda
                     | switch-expr
                     | if-expr
                     | new-expr
                     | array-literal ;                     (* E2 — array syntax not exercised by the corpus *)
                                                           (* E1:parser/ExpressionParser.java parsePrimary *)

(*§6.1*) lambda       = "(" , [ lambda-params ] , ")" , "->" , lambda-body
                     | "{" , [ lambda-params ] , "->" , { statement } , "}" ;
          lambda-params = parameter , { "," , parameter } ;
          lambda-body  = expression | block ;
                                                           (* E1:case functions — "(x: Int) -> x * 2";
                                                              E1:parser/LambdaParser.java,
                                                              ExpressionParser.parseTrailingLambdaBlock *)

(*§6.1*) switch-expr  = "switch" , "(" , expression , ")" , "{" , { switch-arm } , "}" ;
                                                           (* E1:cases enum-switch-expr, switch-expr *)

(*§6.1*) new-expr     = "new" , type-ref , "(" , [ argument-list ] , ")" ;
                                                           (* E1:parser/ExpressionParser.java parseNewExpression *)
```

**Line-sensitive rule (normative for the new frontend).** A trailing lambda
block is attached only when its `{` starts on the **same line** as the token
closing the preceding call. `[E1:parser/ExpressionParser.java
trailingLambdaSameLine]` This is the single place where the legacy grammar
depends on line structure beyond statement separation; a line-insensitive
grammar would silently re-interpret existing programs.

---

## 4. Deliberately absent productions

These are **not** in the grammar. Their absence is a finding, not an omission.

| Absent | Why | Owner |
|---|---|---|
| Triple-quoted / raw / multiline strings | The legacy lexer recognises `"""` only to reject it (`LEX008`) | S8 |
| String interpolation | Explicitly absent per the `LEX008` message; concatenation is `+` | S8 |
| Bitwise complement `~` | Not in the legacy token set; lexes as `LEX005` | S7 |
| Binary, octal, digit-separator and hex-float literals | Not produced by the legacy lexer | S7 |
| `fun` / `fn` / `func` declarations | Reserved-invalid by design (`SG-001`) | settled |
| Package/import wildcards, aliases, static imports | Not observed; the legacy parser reads a plain qualified name | S9 |
| An ownership / borrowing / lifetime syntax | Not evidenced anywhere in the legacy surface | S16 |
| Generics declarations and bounds syntax | Only *use* is evidenced; the declaration form is not derived here | S1 |

---

## 5. Unresolved productions register

| Production | Reason | Record |
|---|---|---|
| `package-decl`, `import-decl` | Optionality, placement, wildcards, aliases | S9 |
| `test-decl`, `application-decl`, `infra-decl` | Outside the corpus; semantics unknown | S10 |
| `extern-decl`, `foreign-module-decl` | Outside the corpus; FFI semantics unknown | S10 |
| `entity-decl` | Keyword reserved; semantics unknown | S10 |
| `annotation` | Vocabulary and semantics unknown | S10 |
| `modifier` combinations | Legal combinations unknown | S10 |
| `type-ref` with nested arguments | Known legacy defect area | S1 |
| `type-ref` `?` semantics | Conversion rules unknown | S2 |
| `parameter` type optionality, default return type | Not evidenced | S1 |
| `for-stmt` three-clause form | Parser-evidenced, corpus-unevidenced | S5 |
| `switch-arm` exhaustiveness, fallthrough, labelled `break`/`continue` | Not evidenced | S5 |
| `assignment-expr` associativity | Not evidenced | S11 |
| `nested-function-decl` capture semantics | Not evidenced | S11 |
| `array-literal`, array indexing details | Not exercised by the corpus | S9 |
| `expression` evaluation order | Never written down; observable only | S11 |
| Identifier class set, normalisation, confusables | Unicode policy open | S13 |
| Trivia and terminator normalisation | Formatter/LSP policy open | S14 |
| Feature-gate syntax for unsupported targets | Not designed | S15 |

---

## 6. Evidence index

| Grammar area | Primary evidence |
|---|---|
| Layout, comments, BOM, terminators, identifiers, literals, operators | `parser/Lexer.java` (`tokenize`, `readString`, `readChar`, `readEscape`, `readUnicodeEscape`, `readNumber`, `readIdentifier`, `readOperatorOrDelimiter`, `skipLineComment`, `skipBlockComment`, `skipTripleQuoted`) |
| Token vocabulary | `TokenType.java` |
| Keyword table, reserved-invalid set | `lang/KofKeywords.java` |
| Compilation unit and top-level dispatch | `parser/Parser.java` (`parse`, `parseTestDeclaration`, `parseApplicationDeclaration`, `parseInfraDeclaration`, `parseFunctionDeclaration`, `parseExternDeclaration`, `parseForeignModule`, `parsePackage`, `parseImports`) |
| Statements | `parser/StatementParser.java` |
| Expressions, precedence | `parser/ExpressionParser.java` |
| Types | `parser/TypeParser.java` |
| Type declarations, members | `parser/TypeDeclarations.java`, `parser/ClassMemberParser.java`, `parser/AnnotationParser.java` |
| Lambdas | `parser/LambdaParser.java` |
| Executed behaviour | `tests/golden/<case>/` — 74 cases, 222/222 executed checks (jvm/js/script) |
